          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-5dd7351a6e0833be\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Christian’s direct “Je t’aime” is principally an avowal presenting his love for Roxane as true. It neither articulates nor independently enacts a commitment, and the deliberate conversational setting supplies no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration occurs within courtship, but courtship and a desire for reciprocation do not by themselves establish performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses Roxane with the highlighted “Je t’aime” during their conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Christian has refused Cyrano’s coaching because he wants to speak for himself, then answers Roxane’s invitation to speak with a blunt declaration of love. Roxane treats it as merely the basic theme and demands elaboration; Christian’s subsequent repetitions reveal limited eloquence rather than expressive/reflexive force. His later request that Roxane say she loves him shows a desire for reciprocation, not a commitment encoded by the highlighted utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After a silence, Christian first says “Je vous aime,” and then gives the highlighted, more intimate “Je t’aime.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is a direct and unqualified presentation of Christian’s loving state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Christian says beforehand, “Je vais parler moi-même,” after refusing to continue borrowing Cyrano’s letters and speeches.\",\"supports\":\"Christian intends this exchange to be his own avowal rather than revoiced language supplied by Cyrano.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane responds, “C’est le thème. Brodez, brodez,” treating “Je t’aime” as the theme that Christian should elaborate.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate uptake identifies the core as a basic declaration of love, not an undertaking or an independently expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Christian continues with “Je t’aime tant” and asks Roxane to tell him that she loves him.\",\"supports\":\"The sequence frames the highlighted words as an avowal coupled with a wish for reciprocal love; it does not supply a promise or relational obligation.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the utterance’s avowal force naturally. Its courtship setting, intimacy, and repetition are contextual features and create no residual pressure requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
